<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuan Dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yuan Dynasty was the Mongol-ruled dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, proclaimed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> in 1271, which completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> in 1279. It made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, its capital, promoted trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. Weakened by factional conflict, floods, plague and rebellion, it was driven from China in 1368 by the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty.jpg" alt="Yuan Dynasty" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cattette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuan_Dynasty_revised.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Kaidu's Ögedeid domains and the Chagatai khan Duwa (r. 1282–1307), whom Kaidu had installed, waged sustained war against the <b>Yuan dynasty</b> of Kublai Khan and his successors in Central Asia, raiding the Uyghur lands and Mongolia and keeping pressure on the Yuan frontier for nearly three decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungars were the leading tribe of the Oirats, the western Mongols, who had formed a loose confederation known as the Four Oirat after the fall of the <b>Yuan dynasty</b> and in the fifteenth century, under Esen Taishi, briefly dominated Mongolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its move of the capital to Zhongdu began Beijing's history as a main imperial capital, a role continued by the <b>Mongol Yuan</b>, the Ming and the Qing; the Lugou Bridge, known in the West as the Marco Polo Bridge, was built near Zhongdu between 1189 and 1192.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mangrai also faced the Mongol <b>Yuan dynasty</b>, which had overrun the Dali kingdom and attacked Pagan; Yuan expeditions sent against Lan Na in the first years of the fourteenth century achieved little, and the kingdom later sent tribute missions to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ports linked south India with the Persian Gulf, the Arab world and <b>Yuan China</b>, and foreign visitors recorded the wealth of its pearl and horse trades: the accounts of Marco Polo and Wassaf are among the most detailed medieval descriptions of a south Indian kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liao was the first of the so-called conquest dynasties, and its combination of steppe and Chinese government influenced the Jin dynasty of the Jurchen, the <b>Mongol Yuan</b> and, through them, the Manchu Qing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
